        "@happycastle/opencode-openmemory - npm\nLocal-first, privacy-focused persistent memory for OpenCode agents using OpenMemory. A fork of opencode-supermemory, redesigned to work with OpenMemory - an open-source, self-hosted cognitive memory engine that keeps your data on your machine.\n > \n@happycastle/opencode-openmemory - npm\nStart using @happycastle/opencode-openmemory in your project by running `npm i @happycastle/opencode-openmemory`. There are no other projects in the npm registry using @happycastle/opencode-openmemory.\n > \n@happycastle/opencode-openmemory - npm\nOpenCode plugin that gives coding agents persistent memory using OpenMemory (local-first, privacy-focused). Latest version: 0.0.3, last published: 4 months ago. Start using @happycastle/opencode-openmemory in your project by running `npm i @happycastle/opencode-openmemory`.\n > \n@happycastle/opencode-openmemory - npm\nLocal-first, privacy-focused persistent memory for OpenCode agents using OpenMemory. A fork of opencode-supermemory, redesigned to work with OpenMemory - an open-source, self-hosted cognitive memory engine that keeps your data on your machine. 1. Install the plugin.\n > \n@happycastle/opencode-openmemory - npm\nIf you're using Oh My OpenCode, disable its built-in auto-compact hook to let this plugin handle context compaction: Add to ~/.config/opencode/oh-my-opencode.json:\n > \n@happycastle/opencode-openmemory - npm\nContext Compaction When the context window fills up (80% by default), the plugin: Injects project knowledge into the summary prompt Triggers OpenCode's summarization Saves the summary as a memory for future sessions Automatically resumes the conversation"
